    "updated_at": "2021-11-17T16:30:04Z",
    "body": "If you're using powerlevel10k with instant prompt, then the prompt will show up immediately and the remaining of the shell startup will happen in the background. Better compare the startup time with a plain prompt, like just `PS1='%~%# '`, disabling all other prompts, just to be sure.\r\n\r\nAlso, I see you're using both prezto and zinit. Not sure if using more than one Zsh framework at the same time will work well.\r\n\r\nAlso, you can profile your whole shell startup by adding the following code snippets at the beginning and end of your `~/.zshrc` file, respectively:\r\n```zsh\r\n###################\r\n#   trace start   #\r\n###################\r\nPS4=$'%D{%s%6.}-_-'\r\nexec 3>&2 2>/tmp/sample-time.$$.log\r\nzmodload zsh/zprof\r\nsetopt xtrace prompt_subst\r\n```\r\n\r\n```zsh\r\n#################\r\n#   trace end   #\r\n#################\r\nunsetopt xtrace\r\nzprof >! /tmp/zprof\r\nlocal line last\r\nwhile IFS= read -r line; do\r\n  if [[ ${line} =~ '^[0-9]+-_-' ]]; then\r\n    if [[ -n ${last} ]]; then\r\n      printf \"%.6f %s\\n\" $(( (${line%%-_-*} - ${last%%-_-*}) / (10.0 ** 6) )) ${last#*-_-}\r\n    fi\r\n    last=${line}\r\n  fi\r\ndone < /tmp/sample-time.(*).log > /tmp/ztrace.log\r\n```\r\n\r\nRestart your terminal, and then specifically, check inside `/tmp/ztrace.log` for the value in the beginning of the line that contains `starship init zsh`. I would be surprised if that value is bigger than a few milliseconds. E.g. I get `0.023010 starship init zsh`.",
