    "body": "Hey there!\n\nI noticed you're on Pop!_OS, which is an Ubuntu derivative. I'm running Ubuntu and I was having an incredibly slow prompt loading lately (>2s in most cases); I subsequently discovered that Ubuntu does something a little irritating - it calls `compinit` in `/etc/zsh/zshrc`, even though it's possible your zsh config is adding other completions and then needs to run `compinit` again. Here's what happens on my system by default (using oh-my-zsh and starship):\n\n```\n$ time zsh -i -c 'true'\nzsh -i -c 'true'  0.74s user 0.79s system 106% cpu 1.444 total\n```\n\n1.5 seconds, which is better than usual honestly. When I add `skip_global_compinit=1` to my `~/.zshenv` (not `~/.zshrc`) and changed nothing else, I got this instead:\n\n```\n$ time zsh -i -c 'true'\nzsh -i -c 'true'  0.18s user 0.18s system 124% cpu 0.297 total\n```\n\nLess than 1/3 of a second. Big change!\n\nI would suggest giving this a try. The difference for me was night and day, so maybe it'll have the same effect for you!",
